candidates.governance_dependencies:
None for the core. These are all permissionless:
- Morpho.createMarket at the enabled IRM and LLTV;
- VaultV2Factory;
- sMUSD deposits and donations;
- PCV.distributeMUSD;
- Morpho liquidate and flashLoan;
- CLPool.flash;
- every BorrowerOperationsSignatures call.

Optional asks to multisig 0x98D8899c3030741925BE630C710A98B57F397C7a, each with a fallback:
(a) PoolsVoter.createVaultGauge for vMUSD, via Mezo's ERC4626VaultAdapter as for the USDC Lending Vault: MEZO to lenders, an interest share to veBTC voters. Fallback: fee-funded MUSD incentives or Merkl.
(b) sMUSD.setStrategy(BufferedLendingStrategy) after audit. Fallback: fee donations through receiveProtocolYield, plus an 'sMUSD → vMUSD' zap holders can opt into.
(c) Mezo's curator allocating USDC Lending Vault mUSDC to M2. Fallback: a small team-seeded mUSDC vault.
(d) enableLltv(0.77e18) on mainnet for a more conservative twin. Fallback: 0.86, with opening LTV ≤ LLTV − 15 pts and liquidity-aware caps.

Correction to two inputs (Baserate and Harbor): whitelisting the vault as a PCV recipient would not free the 14.17M of PCV Stability Pool MUSD. PCV.withdrawFromStabilityPool passes withdrawn MUSD through _repayDebt, which burns it against debtToPay (14.95M) before any recipient is paid (verified in the PCV source). That ask is dropped.

Disclosed:
- The multisig owns Morpho on Mezo and could set a market fee of up to 25% of interest.
- The multisig can upgrade the sMUSD proxy.
- Nothing uses MezoForwarder or ERC-2771.

None. Every call is owner-level, approved or permissionless:
- createBoostGauge (owner or approved);
- BoostVoter and ThirdPartyVoter votes (the account owns the veMEZO);
- addBribes (MUSD is whitelisted);
- pokeBoost/pokeBoosts and PoolsVoter.poke (anyone);
- claims.

Optional:
- The governor's whitelistNFT would let reserve accounts vote after epochVoteEnd. Not needed.
- A governor-created managed veMEZO for the protocol position. Fallback: a normal permanent lock.

Watch items:
- BoostVoter, VeBTC and VeMEZO are upgradeable BUSL proxies owned by multisig 0x98D8…7C7a, so Mezo could change the formula. Settlement reads U and M live.
- Mezo's planned vote delegation could replace EarnAccounts, which helps.
- Halborn 7.4 (bribes are lost if a gauge is killed with no voters): settlement checks isAlive before posting bribes.

fixes:
Inside the settlement tx, check before any seller vote:
- ownerOf(buyer) == bidder;
- unboostedVotingPowerOfNFT > 0, which catches a same-block transfer;
- escrowType NORMAL/MANAGED;
- lock end ≥ epochNext, or permanent;
- isAlive(gauge);
- buyer PoolsVoter pool count ≤ a small N.
Drop failing buyers before voting. Wrap each seller vote and each PoolsVoter.poke in try/catch. Never refund a buyer whose gauge received votes; charge exactly the units measured.

top5.strengths:
It was every judge's #1 (engineer 8.4, business 9.2, tester 8.2; average 8.6) and is still #1 after both red teams (8.2 and 7.8).
- It fills the brief's two top-ranked gaps: nothing lends MUSD, and there is no BTC→MUSD credit free of redemption risk. It also delivers the roadmap's 'broader suite of lending markets'.
- It has the deepest integration in the slate, all on Mezo's own stack:
  - Morpho Blue and Vault V2;
  - sMUSD internals: yieldIndex, claimYield, PCV.distributeMUSD and receiveProtocolYield;
  - Skip with a 60 s staleness bound, plus Pyth;
  - the BTC precompile sent as msg.value (the red team simulated the one-transaction borrow on both networks);
  - Tigris depth and the PoolsVoter gauges.
- The core needs no governance, and the team can run a real loop on mainnet.
- It outputs three ERC-4626 tokens.
- It avoids the mainnet redemption queue that undermines Pylon, mbLP and Keel.

top5.weaknesses:
- **Demand is unproven.** Mezo's Morpho has had 16 borrowers ever. Borrowing MUSD at about 4% only to sell it at 0.989 into an mUSDC bid of about $14k is unattractive.
- **Caps must start very small.** Clean liquidation depth is about 0.1 BTC (CL200 holds 9,956 MUSD), so mainnet caps start at 5–10k MUSD.
- **It is the largest build in the slate.**
- **As first pitched, it had five serious design flaws:**
  - a strategy module that re-entered sMUSD's nonReentrant lock (fatal);
  - position limits enforced only in the router;
  - a vMUSD deposit DoS;
  - a backstop with the wrong economics;
  - over-scoped 'first' claims.
  The champion plan fixes all five.
- **MEZO utility is indirect.** It comes through gauge incentives now and a vault gauge later.

grafted_features:
RiskLens (from C6 Par's PegLens), a read-only lens. It reports:
- MUSD price (Pyth plus the CL10 observe TWAP);
- clean liquidation depth on CL200 (QuoterV2 0x4e56…) and the vAMM (Router.getAmountsOut);
- the cap derivation;
- oracle ages;
- backstop solvency;
- a comparison against the trove redemption queue (SortedTroves, TroveManager.getCurrentICR).
It feeds the depth-linked cap script and the position card.
